@page "/guides/security"

<PageTitle>Content Security Policy · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<DocsArticle Class="docs-article--guide">
    <Contents>
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="the-policy" Text="The policy" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="the-nonce" Text="The nonce" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="what-each-part-allows" Text="What each part allows" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="standalone-webassembly" Text="Standalone WebAssembly" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="dock-panel-windows" Text="Dock panel windows" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="pasted-html" Text="Pasted HTML" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="how-it-is-checked" Text="How it is checked" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="tbthemehead" Text="TbThemeHead" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="related" Text="Related" />
    </Contents>
    <Header>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Content Security Policy</TbText>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
            Trailblazor works under a strict Content Security Policy. It runs no inline scripts and needs no
            <code>'unsafe-eval'</code>, and
            the one <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element it adds takes a nonce. Only style attributes have to be allowed
            inline.
        </TbText>
    </Header>
    <ChildContent>
        <DocsHeading Id="the-policy" Text="The policy" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            This is the strictest policy the library works under. Send it as a <code>Content-Security-Policy</code>
            header on one line, with a new nonce for each response in place of <code>{nonce}</code>, and add your own
            sources, such as a CDN for images, to the parts that need them.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="The minimum policy" Code="@Policy" />

        <DocsHeading Id="the-nonce" Text="The nonce" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            <code>TbThemeHead</code> adds a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> for a seeded palette, and Blazor's
            <code>ImportMap</code> is an inline script, so both take the response's nonce. <code>TbThemeHead</code>
            also puts it on its script. The policy here allows that script through <code>'self'</code>, but a policy
            that uses <code>'strict-dynamic'</code> ignores <code>'self'</code> and needs the nonce. Make the nonce in
            a middleware and pass it to both in <code>App.razor</code>.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Program.cs" Code="@Middleware" />
        <CodeBlock Label="App.razor" Code="@AppHead" />

        <DocsHeading Id="what-each-part-allows" Text="What each part allows" Class="docs-section" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong><code>style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'</code></strong>: components set layout values, such as a
                panel's size or a popup's position, in style attributes as they render. The stylesheets themselves
                stay under <code>style-src</code>, so no inline <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> applies without the nonce.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>'wasm-unsafe-eval'</code></strong>: only for WebAssembly, which compiles the app's code.
                A Blazor Server app can leave it out.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>connect-src 'self'</code></strong>: Blazor Server's circuit is a WebSocket to the app's
                own host, which <code>'self'</code> covers.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>img-src data: blob:</code></strong>: images the library builds in or makes in the
                browser: <code>data:</code> URLs for a watermark's tile and the time field's clock icon, and a
                <code>blob:</code> URL for a signature pad's image.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <DocsHeading Id="standalone-webassembly" Text="Standalone WebAssembly" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            A standalone WebAssembly app is served as static files, which can't take a new nonce for each response.
            Put a palette's CSS in a stylesheet of your own instead, as the
            <a href="guides/theming#custom-palette">Theming</a> guide shows, and allow the inline scripts of your
            <code>index.html</code> by their hashes in <code>script-src</code>. The import map's hash changes whenever
            the build fingerprints the assets again, so update it with each release.
        </TbText>

        <DocsHeading Id="dock-panel-windows" Text="Dock panel windows" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            A <a href="components/dock-manager">dock manager</a> panel opened in a window of its own is an
            <code>about:blank</code> window, which inherits the page's policy. The panel's styles are copies of the
            page's, each <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> with the nonce the page first loaded with, even after an enhanced
            navigation has brought a new one, so the same policy covers the window.
        </TbText>

        <DocsHeading Id="pasted-html" Text="Pasted HTML" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            The <a href="components/rich-text-editor">rich text editor</a> reads pasted HTML in an inert document, so
            nothing in it runs or loads. Chromium still reports the inline handlers and styles it finds there, such as
            those in a page copied from Word, as violations from <code>rich-text-editor.js</code>. If your policy
            sends reports, expect these, and leave them out of any alert.
        </TbText>

        <DocsHeading Id="how-it-is-checked" Text="How it is checked" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            Every week, the library's browser tests run in Chromium under this policy, and a test fails if the
            library's scripts cause any violation in its page or a window it opens, apart from what the rich text
            editor reports while it reads HTML. Tests that add inline styles or handlers of their own skip that run.
            Another test checks that a style with the nonce applies on the page and in a dock panel's window, and that
            the page reports nothing at all. A unit test also renders each component on its own, statically and
            interactively, and checks that none writes a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element, an inline script or an
            inline handler.
        </TbText>

        <DocsHeading Id="tbthemehead" Text="TbThemeHead" Class="docs-section" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(TbThemeHead)" />

        <DocsHeading Id="related" Text="Related" Class="docs-section" />
        <ul>
            <li><a href="guides/getting-started">Getting started</a>: where <code>TbThemeHead</code> goes.</li>
            <li><a href="guides/theming">Theming</a>: seeded palettes, which add the <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <GuideFooter Page="GetType()" />
    </ChildContent>
</DocsArticle>

@code {
    private static readonly string Policy =
        string.Join(";" + Environment.NewLine, ContentSecurityPolicy.Directives("{nonce}"));

    private const string Middleware = """
        using System.Security.Cryptography;

        app.Use((context, next) =>
        {
            var nonce = Convert.ToBase64String(RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(16));
            context.Items["csp-nonce"] = nonce;
            context.Response.Headers.ContentSecurityPolicy = Policy(nonce);
            return next();
        });

        // The policy above, on one line with the nonce in it.
        static string Policy(string nonce) => string.Join("; ",
            "default-src 'self'",
            $"script-src 'self' 'nonce-{nonce}' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'",
            $"style-src 'self' 'nonce-{nonce}'",
            "style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'",
            "img-src 'self' data: blob:",
            "connect-src 'self'",
            "object-src 'none'",
            "base-uri 'self'",
            "form-action 'self'");
        """;

    private const string AppHead = """
        <head>
            ...
            <TbStyles />
            <TbThemeHead Nonce="@Nonce" />
            <ImportMap nonce="@Nonce" />
            <HeadOutlet />
        </head>

        @code {
            [CascadingParameter]
            private HttpContext HttpContext { get; set; } = default!;

            private string? Nonce => HttpContext.Items["csp-nonce"] as string;
        }
        """;
}
